  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to novium for supporting \nPBS. What does an electron really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look like? I mean if we zoom in all the \nway is it a sizeless speck of charge? Or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is it a multi-dimensional vortex \nof quantum strangeness? Is it the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15680,
    "dDurationMs": 5963,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boundary of a tiny universe with universe \nelectrons of its own? Let's find out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There isn't much that's simpler than the it's \none of the elementary particles something not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made of smaller things at least according to the \ncurrent theory. It conjures to mind a single point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or perhaps a tiny ball made of some electric \ncharge and mass and not much else - like in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the cartoon description of the atom with electrons \nas orbiting balls. But if you've paid attention to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good pop-sci media you probably know that these \npictures aren't accurate. Perhaps you know that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56440,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in quantum mechanics particles exist not as tiny \nnuggets of matter but rather as wave functions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fuzzy distributions that define the probability of \nwhere or what the particle might be when measured.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69920,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example electrons in atoms are more accurately \ndepicted as blurred shells than as orbiting balls  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77600,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a free electron might look like an undulating \nwave packet capable of interfering with itself and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84040,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doing other wave-like things. But are these \nwave functions really what an electron looks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like? These wavy structures seem more intuitive \nfor something like the photon which is literally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a wave in the electromagnetic field and whose most \nimportant properties like frequency and wavelength  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be captured in wavy terms. But an electron \nhas mass, it has electric charge, it has spin.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108960,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where do these properties live in a fuzzy wave \nfunction? There are valiant efforts to represent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116120,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the non-spatial components of the electron wave \nfunction in particular the spin or wave function  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we talk about here but these are attempts \nto visualize a pretty abstract concept not really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126880,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what the electron looks like. So let's take a \nlook. Let's really zoom in. We can never actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see a fuzzy wave packet because the electron takes \non precise properties when we measure it. Looking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141880,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the electron means resolving its location and \nspatial structure as we measure more precisely the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147920,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wave function shrinks to something particle like. \nBut how far can we zoom? Do we eventually see a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155400,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny ball or an infintesmal speck of charge and \nmass? And where does that charge and mass live?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162920,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does an electron really look like up close? \nThere's an agenda behind this thought experiment.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170080,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The very act of zooming in to see what an electron \nlooks like will reveal some vexing contradictions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example about why the electron has the \nproperties that it has. These contradictions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "foreshadow one of the biggest problems in \nphysics today but that's for future episodes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we're just looking at the electron. Let's \ntry constructing an electron first using classical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194760,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics. We'll do that by gathering enough \nelectric charge from the surrounding universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199680,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sticking it together. Like charge repels via \nthe Coulomb force and Coulomb's law tells us that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closer we want to drag two bits of charge the more \nforce it takes. That means it takes more energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to assemble a more compact ball of charge than it \ndoes a more diffused one assuming the same amount  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217760,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of charge. A bit of calculus gives us the energy \nneeded to assemble a ball of any given radius and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225040,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is the potential energy that that ball holds \nafter assembled the smaller the ball the more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231520,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy from Einstein's relativity we know that \nbound energy is equivalent to mass e =mc^2. So now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240480,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can ask the question if we drag in one electron \nworth of electric charge until it's in a small  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough ball that its potential energy is equal \nto the known mass of the electron how big would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253840,
    "dDurationMs": 10640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that ball be well around 2.8 by 10 ^ -5 M. This \nis the classical electron radius it's the size  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264480,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an electron would have to be if its mass was all \nfrom the potential energy of it contain charge.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it's not the real size of the electron. \nOur experiments tell us that the true size of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277880,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron is smaller than 10 ^ -7 M at least \n100 times smaller than the classical electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285920,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radius. But if we continue to crush our ball of \ncharge down to that size the mass equivalence of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292040,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that potential energy is 10G electron volts which \nis 20,000 times larger than the actual measured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300760,
    "dDurationMs": 9440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron mass of 511 kilo electron volts and if \nour electron really is point like, having no size  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then Coulumb's law gets a zero in the denominator \nand the energy summed all the way to that central  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316040,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point is infinite. Now the electron isn't really a \nball of electric charge mutually repelling itself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its charge does self- interact in complex \nways manifested in the activity of quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329960,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field surrounding electron as we'll see but the \nequations and numbers I came up with do still give  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337440,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy contained in the electromagnetic field \nthat emanates from a bundle of charge of the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343480,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size. And so this energy does still contribute \nto the electron observed mass. This issue of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350880,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron's electric field containing vastly more \nmass than the electron itself is or at least was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357640,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a real problem and one that persists even when \nwe switch from classical to quantum mechanics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for now we get back to the electron what \ndoes our zoom in look like when we switch to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370240,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics or more precisely quantum field \ntheory? In qft we describe electromagnetism in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377400,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms of the activity of a quantum field and each \nelementary particle is an excitation in its own  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum field. So the photon is an excitation of \nthe EM field and the electron and positron are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389200,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "excitations of the electron field. Quantum fields \nare sometimes described as these roiling oceans of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395160,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual particles appearing and vanishing. This \nisn't very accurate. Instead think of a quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field as having the potential for complex \nand random fluctuations when it encounters a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405680,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real particle whose fluctuations can be modeled \nusing virtual particles. As a mathematical tool  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can model the coulomb force in terms of virtual \nphotons in the EM field imparting momentum between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418600,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charged particles or we can model the spontaneous \ntransfer of energy between the em and electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425200,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields in terms of a virtual photon turning into \nvirtual matter anti pairs pair production. In this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432800,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case a virtual electron and positron. So if the \nEM field in quantum field theory is an ocean of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439800,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual photons at least mathematically. What does \nthat look like? Well viewed from afar the activity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446480,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these virtual photons is averaged over in \ninto the smooth field described by classical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetism but as our zoom in reaches \na certain point around 100 times larger than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458680,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the classical electron radius we transition to a \nregime where we can't ignore the quantum behavior  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466360,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the field. For example as the field strengthens \nand our view narrows we see that the interaction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472600,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the em and electron fields strengthens. \nWe can describe this as an increase in the rate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478640,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of pair production an increasingly active \nflickering of virtual photons and the virtual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron positron pairs that they spawn this cloud \nof activity appears to grow stronger the closer we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491160,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get to the electron. It's not just that we're \nseeing the quantum properties of the stronger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496080,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "em field - the very act of probing the electron \nthis closely amplifies quantum fluctuations. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heisenberg uncertainty principle tells us that \nprecision in spatial or temporal measurement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509520,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leads to larger uncertainty in momentum and energy \nrespectively the more precise our snapshot of that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517560,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron the more energetic quantum fluctuations we'll observe. We talk about this effect in more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525160,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detail in this video but the upshot is that \nthe universe looks very different depending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531040,
    "dDurationMs": 8680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the scale that it's probed and so does the \nelectron although somehow all scales knit together  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539720,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a unified whole. We are now at very small \nscales quite a bit smaller than the classical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544960,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radius here. It's clear that the bare electron \nappears to be dressed in this cloud of virtual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551960,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "activity this dressing is the source of our self \nenergy and the extra mass from the electron's EM  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558080,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. As we zoom ever closer to the electron \nthe energy in the field approaches the rest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564200,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass of the electron and a new interaction becomes \nimportant. Now the virtual electron positron pairs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572560,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have a decent likelihood of directly interacting \nwith the central real electron. Sometimes a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579440,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual positron will actually annihilate with \nthe real electron which then promotes its once  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585720,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual partner electron to reality, it's as \nthough the original electron shifted position.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two important implications to this. \nEffect one is that this effect actually recueses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the electron from having the colossal or even \ninfinite mass predicted by classical physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605120,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More on that next time. Unfortunately the second \nimplication of this self annihilation interaction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612400,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that we're never actually going to get a clear \nview of the electron no matter how far we zoom in.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620120,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The closer we zoom the stronger the surrounding \ncloud of virtual fluctuations but also the more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627960,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequent these interactions become. At some point \nthe electron become this flickering blur that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635120,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could never quite fix the location of the mass \nends up smeared out both by the self energy mass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643280,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and by what is ultimately Heisenberg uncertainty. \nThe electron's charge is also smeared out by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651360,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "latter and also smeared by something that I \nhaven't told you about yet. Even before we got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657560,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close to the central electron virtual electron \npositron pairs we're smearing out that charge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664000,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those pairs interact with the ambient em field \nwith the positrons tending to shift closer to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671400,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "center while virtual electrons shift further away. \nThis so-called vacuum polarization happens all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the central electron and has the effect of \nscreening the external universe from that central  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685400,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge. In fact the observed charge should appear \nto rise as we get closer to the center past this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693120,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screening. If you calculate the effective charge \nscreening all the way to infinitesimal distances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699800,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that central charge has to be infinite - that \nmeans the central electron would need to have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705360,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite charge itself to overcome that infinite \ncharge screening. Of course we now know that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710520,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can never probe the electron down to a perfectly \nlocalized point so that infinity is probably  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaningless. However if it is true that the \nstrength of the electromagnetic force increases at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extremely small distances when charge screening is \novercome even though we can't catch our electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in our infinite zoom the elusive particle is \nextremely well understood at least mathematically.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We even know how to rescue the electron from \nhaving its mass and charge explode due to its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interaction with the surrounding quantum fields - \nalthough to understand that we need a tool called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746920,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "renormalization which we'll explore soon. And \nwe'll also see how this tricky issue with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753040,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron foreshadowed a much much bigger conundrum \nin physics - the hierarchy problem. And will meet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759800,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particle that has yet to be rescued from this \nissue. But that is also another story. For now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767480,
    "dDurationMs": 8200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let's admire the tiny electron outwardly simple \nbut inwardly mercurial and it's a strangeness that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775680,
    "dDurationMs": 8360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only compounds as we localize it in SpaceTime. \nThank you to Novium for supporting PBS. Inspired  "
    } ]
  }, {
